{
  "audit": "licence closure of the installed scripts/checks tree (design row 18; C4, C38; group E2): the opentimestamps client and the render check's playwright-core",
  "date": "2026-10-17",
  "tree": "npm ci --prefix scripts/checks --ignore-scripts from package-lock.json (lockfileVersion 3), npm 11.8.0, node 24.13.1",
  "method": "every package the lockfile installs, with its lockfile licence and sha512 integrity, its licence file (SHA256) and its copyright line; no package declares an install script (and --ignore-scripts runs none); no native (.node) or wasm binary in the tree; tests/test_ots.py re-derives the package list from package-lock.json and checks the installed licence files when the tree is present",
  "direct": {"opentimestamps": "0.4.9", "playwright-core": "1.59.1"},
  "licences": ["Apache-2.0", "BSD-2-Clause", "BSD-3-Clause", "ISC", "LGPL-3.0", "MIT", "MIT-style", "Unlicense"],
  "counts": {"Apache-2.0": 9, "BSD-2-Clause": 1, "BSD-3-Clause": 4, "ISC": 11, "LGPL-3.0": 1, "MIT": 61, "MIT-style": 1, "Unlicense": 1},
  "result": "closed: 89 packages, all permissive (MIT, ISC, BSD-2-Clause, BSD-3-Clause, Apache-2.0, Unlicense, the MIT-style jsbn notice) except opentimestamps 0.4.9 (LGPL-3.0). opentimestamps is installed unmodified by npm ci, never vendored, copied or bundled, and is called as a library by scripts/ots.mjs; the LGPL-3.0 allows that use from code under any licence, including the repository LICENSE now and AGPL-3.0 (design row 22). Its notice is in scripts/checks/NOTICE. None of the tree is published on the site",
  "advisories": {"run": "npm audit --prefix scripts/checks, 2026-10-18 against the npm registry advisories: 11 packages flagged (2 critical, 8 moderate, 1 low, 0 high); every one comes in through opentimestamps 0.4.9, and npm offers no fix but a downgrade to opentimestamps 0.0.0, so none is applied (the pin stands, design Risk 13)", "findings": [{"package": "request@2.88.2", "severity": "critical", "advisories": ["GHSA-p8p7-x288-28g6"], "title": "Server-Side Request Forgery (redirects followed across hosts and protocols)"}, {"package": "form-data@2.3.3", "severity": "critical", "advisories": ["GHSA-fjxv-7rqg-78g4", "GHSA-hmw2-7cc7-3qxx"], "title": "unsafe random multipart boundary; CRLF injection in multipart field names"}, {"package": "qs@6.5.5", "severity": "moderate", "advisories": ["GHSA-6rw7-vpxm-498p", "GHSA-4mjr-xmp4-gh2g"], "title": "denial of service through attacker-built query strings"}, {"package": "tough-cookie@2.5.0", "severity": "moderate", "advisories": ["GHSA-72xf-g2v4-qvf3"], "title": "prototype pollution through cookies"}, {"package": "uuid@3.4.0", "severity": "moderate", "advisories": ["GHSA-w5hq-g745-h8pq"], "title": "missing buffer bounds check in v3/v5/v6 with a caller buffer"}, {"package": "bn.js@4.11.8 and 4.12.5", "severity": "moderate", "advisories": ["GHSA-378v-28hj-76wf"], "title": "infinite loop"}, {"package": "elliptic@6.6.1", "severity": "low", "advisories": ["GHSA-848j-6mx2-7j84"], "title": "risky cryptographic primitive implementation"}, {"package": "bitcore-lib, request-promise, request-promise-core, opentimestamps", "severity": "moderate", "advisories": [], "title": "flagged only for depending on the packages above"}], "scope": "the client runs only on the GitHub runner, in the refresh job steps \"OpenTimestamps proof of the day's manifest\" and \"OpenTimestamps upgrade and verify\" (and offline in the tests), never in the browser or on the site; those steps carry no secret or token in their environment (persist-credentials: false, no GH_TOKEN, no id-token) and are time-capped; scripts/ots.mjs contacts only the client's four default aggregators when stamping, the calendars a proof names that pass the client's default whitelist (*.calendar.opentimestamps.org, *.calendar.eternitywall.com, *.calendar.catallaxy.com) when upgrading, and https://blockstream.info/api when verifying, with a deadline per call; it sends a SHA256 or a block height and reads a proof or a block header, so a redirect (request SSRF) can reach nothing that holds a credential; no multipart body is built (form-data), no query object comes from a response (qs), no cookie jar is enabled (tough-cookie), no uuid is made with a caller buffer (uuid); bitcore-lib, bn.js and elliptic are reached only by the client's printable tree (Timestamp.strTree and toJson), which scripts/ots.mjs never calls, and no key or signature is made"},
